IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ea95-claimant-days`

-- Evidence Act 1995 (Commonwealth of Australia) -- what a person bringing a civil claim meets: whether this Act governs the
-- evidence in my claim at all (s 4, the Dictionary's "federal court" and "civil proceeding"); the standard I must prove my
-- case to (s 140); using what someone else said or wrote (ss 59, 60, 62, 63, 64, 66A, 67, 68, Dictionary Part 2 cl 4), and
-- business records (s 69); opinion and expert evidence, and expert certificates (ss 76, 78, 79, 80, 177); the other side's
-- conviction, and a grant of probate (ss 91, 92); tendency and coincidence evidence in a civil claim (ss 94, 97, 98, 100);
-- my lawyer's advice and the documents prepared for my claim (ss 118, 119, 120, 122; ss 121, 123-126 as one fact); what was
-- said in settlement negotiations (s 131); the court's general discretions (ss 135, 136); when a letter I posted, or an
-- email I sent, is presumed received (ss 160, 161); requests to the other side about their evidence, and what happens if
-- they ignore them (ss 166-169); waiving the rules of evidence and agreed facts (ss 190, 191).
--
-- SEAT: the claimant -- the person who brings a civil claim (the plaintiff or applicant) in a court to which this Act
-- applies: a claim for compensation or damages, an employment, discrimination or consumer claim, a superannuation or
-- insurance claim, a class action, a claim on a deceased estate. "Can I use what the witness told me? Can my doctor's report
-- go in without the doctor? Can I use the driver's conviction? Can they see what I said to my lawyer? Can my settlement
-- offer be used against me? By when must I object to their notice?" Provisions addressed to the court are encoded as what
-- they mean for that person. Every finding, discretion or satisfaction the Act leaves to the court is asked as an input
-- ("the court finds ..."), never guessed.
--
-- Edition: the compilation registered as C2025C00340 (Compilation No. 35, compilation date 10 June 2025; Federal Register of
-- Legislation, retrieved 13 October 2026), as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/C2004A04858.txt.
--
-- This encoding sits beside baseline-2026-10 (the accused's seat, criminal proceedings), which it does not touch.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written from the claimant's seat with the 2026-10-18 defaults. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Evidence Act 1995 -- the person bringing a civil claim`

§§ `Questions this encoding does not cover`

-- The stable l4 has no REFUSE. A question that can reach a gap answers with an `Answer`: either `answered` with the value,
-- or `not covered` with the reason.
DECLARE `Why my claim is not covered` IS ONE OF
    -- s 4(1), Dictionary "federal court"; Judiciary Act 1903 s 79 (s 4 Note 4)
    `my claim is in a State or Territory court: the evidence law of that State or Territory applies to it (for a claim in federal jurisdiction, picked up by s 79 of the Judiciary Act 1903, s 4 Note 4), and that law is not encoded here`

GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH A TYPE
DECLARE `Claim answer` a IS ONE OF
    `answered for my claim` HAS `the answer for my claim` IS AN a
    `not covered for my claim` HAS `the reason it is not covered` IS A `Why my claim is not covered`

§§ `s 4, Dictionary -- does this Act govern the evidence in my claim`

-- s 4(1): "This Act applies to all proceedings in a federal court, including proceedings that: ... (b) are interlocutory
-- proceedings or proceedings of a similar kind; or (c) are heard in chambers". Dictionary: "federal court means: (a) the High
-- Court; or (b) any other court created by the Parliament (other than the Supreme Court of a Territory); and includes a
-- person or body (other than a court or magistrate of a State or Territory) that, in performing a function or exercising a
-- power under a law of the Commonwealth, is required to apply the laws of evidence." "civil proceeding means a proceeding
-- other than a criminal proceeding." s 4(5): the Act (other than ss 185-187) does not apply to "(a) an appeal from a court of
-- a State ...; or (b) an appeal from a court of the Australian Capital Territory, the Northern Territory or an external
-- Territory; or (e) a review of a decision or order of a magistrate and any appeal from such a review". s 4(5A): "Despite
-- subsection (5), this Act applies to an appeal to the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1) from a
-- court of summary jurisdiction of a State or Territory exercising jurisdiction under the Family Law Act 1975."
DECLARE `Does this Act govern my claim` IS ONE OF
    `yes: my claim is a civil proceeding in a federal court, and this Act applies to all proceedings in a federal court, interlocutory and chambers hearings included (s 4(1); Dictionary "federal court", "civil proceeding")`
    `yes: this is an appeal to the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1) from a court of summary jurisdiction exercising Family Law Act jurisdiction, to which this Act applies despite s 4(5) (s 4(5A))`
    `no: this Act (other than ss 185-187) does not apply to an appeal from a State or Territory court or to a review of a magistrate's decision or order (s 4(5))`

@export Does the Commonwealth Evidence Act 1995 govern the evidence in my civil claim?
GIVEN `my claim is in a federal court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is being heard in the High Court or another court created by the federal Parliament (for example the Federal Court of Australia or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia), or by a federal body that must apply the laws of evidence, and not in a State or Territory court or tribunal (a local or magistrates court, a District or County Court, or a State or Territory Supreme Court)
      `it is an appeal from a State or Territory court or a review of a magistrate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the hearing is an appeal from a court of a State or Territory (or of an external Territory), or a review of a magistrate's decision or order (or an appeal from such a review)
      `it is a family law appeal from a court of summary jurisdiction to the Federal Circuit and Family Court Division 1` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the hearing is an appeal to the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1) from a State or Territory court of summary jurisdiction exercising jurisdiction under the Family Law Act 1975
GIVETH A `Claim answer` `Does this Act govern my claim`
`Does this Act govern my claim question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `my claim is in a federal court`)
    THEN `not covered for my claim` `my claim is in a State or Territory court: the evidence law of that State or Territory applies to it (for a claim in federal jurisdiction, picked up by s 79 of the Judiciary Act 1903, s 4 Note 4), and that law is not encoded here`
    ELSE IF `it is an appeal from a State or Territory court or a review of a magistrate`
         THEN IF `it is a family law appeal from a court of summary jurisdiction to the Federal Circuit and Family Court Division 1`
              THEN `answered for my claim` `yes: this is an appeal to the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1) from a court of summary jurisdiction exercising Family Law Act jurisdiction, to which this Act applies despite s 4(5) (s 4(5A))`
              ELSE `answered for my claim` `no: this Act (other than ss 185-187) does not apply to an appeal from a State or Territory court or to a review of a magistrate's decision or order (s 4(5))`
         ELSE `answered for my claim` `yes: my claim is a civil proceeding in a federal court, and this Act applies to all proceedings in a federal court, interlocutory and chambers hearings included (s 4(1); Dictionary "federal court", "civil proceeding")`

§§ `s 140 -- what I must prove my claim to`

-- s 140(1): "In a civil proceeding, the court must find the case of a party proved if it is satisfied that the case has been
-- proved on the balance of probabilities." (2): "it is to take into account: (a) the nature of the cause of action or
-- defence; and (b) the nature of the subject-matter of the proceeding; and (c) the gravity of the matters alleged." Which
-- party bears the burden of proving which fact is not in this Act: it comes from the law that gives the claim or defence.
DECLARE `Must the court find my case proved` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the court is satisfied on the balance of probabilities, so it must find my case proved (s 140(1))`
    `no: s 140(1) requires the court to find my case proved only if it is satisfied on the balance of probabilities, taking into account the nature of the cause of action, the subject-matter and the gravity of what is alleged (s 140(2)); who bears the burden on each fact is for the law that gives the claim`

@export If the court thinks my claim is more likely than not proved, must it find for me on it?
GIVEN `the court is satisfied my case is proved on the balance of probabilities` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that your case is proved on the balance of probabilities (more likely than not), having taken into account the nature of your claim, its subject-matter and the gravity of what you allege
GIVETH A `Must the court find my case proved`
`Must the court find my case proved question` MEANS
    IF `the court is satisfied my case is proved on the balance of probabilities`
    THEN `yes: the court is satisfied on the balance of probabilities, so it must find my case proved (s 140(1))`
    ELSE `no: s 140(1) requires the court to find my case proved only if it is satisfied on the balance of probabilities, taking into account the nature of the cause of action, the subject-matter and the gravity of what is alleged (s 140(2)); who bears the burden on each fact is for the law that gives the claim`

§§ `ss 59-68, Dictionary Part 2 cl 4 -- using what someone else said or wrote (if I want to rely on it)`

-- s 59(1): evidence of a previous representation "is not admissible to prove the existence of a fact that it can reasonably
-- be supposed that the person intended to assert by the representation". s 60(1): "The hearsay rule does not apply to
-- evidence of a previous representation that is admitted because it is relevant for a purpose other than proof of an
-- asserted fact." s 66A: the hearsay rule "does not apply to evidence of a previous representation made by a person if the
-- representation was a contemporaneous representation about the person's health, feelings, sensations, intention,
-- knowledge or state of mind." s 62: Division 2 is about representations made "by a person who had personal knowledge of an
-- asserted fact" (saw, heard or otherwise perceived it).
-- s 63 (maker not available, civil): the hearsay rule does not apply to "(a) evidence of the representation that is given by
-- a person who saw, heard or otherwise perceived the representation being made; or (b) a document so far as it contains the
-- representation". s 64(2) (maker available, civil): the same, "if it would cause undue expense or undue delay, or would
-- not be reasonably practicable, to call the person who made the representation". s 64(3): if the maker "has been or is to
-- be called", evidence of it by that person or a perceiver; (4): a document "must not be tendered before the conclusion of
-- the examination in chief of the person who made the representation, unless the court gives leave".
-- s 67(1): ss 63(2) and 64(2) "do not apply to evidence adduced by a party unless that party has given reasonable notice in
-- writing to each other party"; (4): the court may direct that they apply despite the failure. s 68(1): after a s 64(2)
-- notice a party may object "not later than 21 days after notice has been given".
-- Dictionary Part 2 cl 4(1): a person is "not ... available" if "(a) the person is dead; or (b) ... not competent to give the
-- evidence; or (c) ... mentally or physically unable to give the evidence and it is not reasonably practicable to overcome
-- that inability; or (d) it would be unlawful for the person to give the evidence; or (e) a provision of this Act prohibits
-- the evidence being given; or (f) all reasonable steps have been taken ... to find the person or secure his or her
-- attendance, but without success; or (g) all reasonable steps have been taken ... to compel the person to give the
-- evidence, but without success." (2): "In all other cases the person is taken to be available".
DECLARE `How can I use the statement` IS ONE OF
    `the hearsay rule does not apply: I use it for a purpose other than proving the fact the person asserted (s 60(1)), and once admitted it may be used to prove that fact too`
    `the hearsay rule does not apply: it was a contemporaneous statement by the person about their own health, feelings, sensations, intention, knowledge or state of mind (s 66A)`
    `the hearsay rule does not apply: the person is not available and the evidence is first-hand, from someone who perceived the statement being made or a document containing it (s 63(2)), and I have given notice or the court has directed (s 67)`
    `not yet: the person is not available and s 63(2) would apply, but only once I give reasonable written notice to each other party or the court directs that it applies despite no notice (s 67(1), (4))`
    `the hearsay rule does not apply: the person has been or will be called as a witness, so the person or someone who perceived the statement may give evidence of it (s 64(3)); a document containing it cannot be tendered before the end of the person's examination in chief without the court's leave (s 64(4))`
    `the hearsay rule does not apply: calling the person would cause undue expense or delay or would not be reasonably practicable (s 64(2)), and I have given notice or the court has directed (s 67); the other side may object within 21 days of my notice (s 68)`
    `not yet: s 64(2) would apply, but only once I give reasonable written notice to each other party, stating that I rely on s 64(2) and on which ground, or the court directs that it applies despite no notice (s 67(1), (3), (4))`
    `no exception in Division 2 on these facts: the person is available, is not being called, and calling them is not shown to cause undue expense or delay or to be impracticable (s 64); call the person, or rely on another exception such as business records (s 69)`
    `no exception in Division 2 on these facts: the statement is not first-hand (the person lacked personal knowledge, s 62), or my evidence of it is not from someone who perceived it being made or a document containing it (ss 63(2), 64(2)); another exception, such as business records (s 69), may apply`

@export Can I use what someone else said or wrote (hearsay) to prove a fact in my civil claim?
GIVEN `I use it to prove the fact the person asserted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you want to use the statement to prove that what the person said was true (FALSE if you use it for another purpose, for example to show that the statement was made, or what someone knew)
      `it was a statement at the time about their own health, feelings, sensations, intention, knowledge or state of mind` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the statement was made by the person at the time, about their own health, feelings, sensations (such as pain), intention, knowledge or state of mind
      `the person had personal knowledge of the fact` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who made the statement saw, heard or otherwise perceived the fact themselves (not just heard about it from someone else)
      `my evidence is from someone who perceived the statement being made, or a document containing it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the evidence of the statement will come from someone who saw or heard the statement being made, or from a document that contains it (FALSE if it would be someone who only heard about the statement second-hand)
      `the person has died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who made the statement has died
      `the person cannot lawfully or competently give the evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person is not competent to give the evidence, is mentally or physically unable to give it and that cannot reasonably be overcome, or giving it would be unlawful or is prohibited by the Evidence Act
      `all reasonable steps to find or compel the person have failed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have taken all reasonable steps to find the person, secure their attendance or compel them to give the evidence, without success
      `the person has been or will be called as a witness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who made the statement has been or will be called to give evidence in your claim
      `the court finds calling the person would cause undue expense or delay or be impracticable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether calling the person would cause undue expense or undue delay, or would not be reasonably practicable (as the court finds, if the other side disputes it)
      `I gave each other party reasonable written notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have given each other party reasonable notice in writing that you intend to use the statement, stating the section relied on (and, for s 64(2), the ground)
      `the court has directed the exception applies despite no notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court, on your application, has directed that the exception applies even though you did not give notice
GIVETH A `How can I use the statement`
`How can I use the statement question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `I use it to prove the fact the person asserted`)
    THEN `the hearsay rule does not apply: I use it for a purpose other than proving the fact the person asserted (s 60(1)), and once admitted it may be used to prove that fact too`
    ELSE IF `it was a statement at the time about their own health, feelings, sensations, intention, knowledge or state of mind`
         THEN `the hearsay rule does not apply: it was a contemporaneous statement by the person about their own health, feelings, sensations, intention, knowledge or state of mind (s 66A)`
         ELSE IF (NOT `the person had personal knowledge of the fact`) OR (NOT `my evidence is from someone who perceived the statement being made, or a document containing it`)
              THEN IF `the person has been or will be called as a witness` AND `the person had personal knowledge of the fact`
                   THEN `the hearsay rule does not apply: the person has been or will be called as a witness, so the person or someone who perceived the statement may give evidence of it (s 64(3)); a document containing it cannot be tendered before the end of the person's examination in chief without the court's leave (s 64(4))`
                   ELSE `no exception in Division 2 on these facts: the statement is not first-hand (the person lacked personal knowledge, s 62), or my evidence of it is not from someone who perceived it being made or a document containing it (ss 63(2), 64(2)); another exception, such as business records (s 69), may apply`
              ELSE IF `the person is not available`
                   THEN IF `notice is answered`
                        THEN `the hearsay rule does not apply: the person is not available and the evidence is first-hand, from someone who perceived the statement being made or a document containing it (s 63(2)), and I have given notice or the court has directed (s 67)`
                        ELSE `not yet: the person is not available and s 63(2) would apply, but only once I give reasonable written notice to each other party or the court directs that it applies despite no notice (s 67(1), (4))`
                   ELSE IF `the person has been or will be called as a witness`
                        THEN `the hearsay rule does not apply: the person has been or will be called as a witness, so the person or someone who perceived the statement may give evidence of it (s 64(3)); a document containing it cannot be tendered before the end of the person's examination in chief without the court's leave (s 64(4))`
                        ELSE IF `the court finds calling the person would cause undue expense or delay or be impracticable`
                             THEN IF `notice is answered`
                                  THEN `the hearsay rule does not apply: calling the person would cause undue expense or delay or would not be reasonably practicable (s 64(2)), and I have given notice or the court has directed (s 67); the other side may object within 21 days of my notice (s 68)`
                                  ELSE `not yet: s 64(2) would apply, but only once I give reasonable written notice to each other party, stating that I rely on s 64(2) and on which ground, or the court directs that it applies despite no notice (s 67(1), (3), (4))`
                             ELSE `no exception in Division 2 on these facts: the person is available, is not being called, and calling them is not shown to cause undue expense or delay or to be impracticable (s 64); call the person, or rely on another exception such as business records (s 69)`
    WHERE
        `the person is not available` MEANS
               `the person has died`
            OR `the person cannot lawfully or competently give the evidence`
            OR `all reasonable steps to find or compel the person have failed`
        `notice is answered` MEANS
            `I gave each other party reasonable written notice` OR `the court has directed the exception applies despite no notice`

§§ `s 69 -- business records (if I want to rely on one)`

-- s 69(1): a document that "is or forms part of the records belonging to or kept by a person, body or organisation in the
-- course of, or for the purposes of, a business" (or once was) and "contains a previous representation made or recorded in
-- the document in the course of, or for the purposes of, the business". (2): the hearsay rule does not apply "if the
-- representation was made: (a) by a person who had or might reasonably be supposed to have had personal knowledge of the
-- asserted fact; or (b) on the basis of information directly or indirectly supplied by" such a person. (3): "Subsection (2)
-- does not apply if the representation: (a) was prepared or obtained for the purpose of conducting, or for or in
-- contemplation of or in connection with, an Australian or overseas proceeding; or (b) was made in connection with an
-- investigation relating or leading to a criminal proceeding." Dictionary Part 2 cl 1: "business" includes a profession,
-- calling, occupation, trade or undertaking, the Crown's activities, and a business not carried on for profit.
DECLARE `Can I use the business record` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the hearsay rule does not apply to the record so far as it contains the statement (s 69(2))`
    `no: s 69(2) does not apply, because the statement was prepared or obtained for, or in contemplation of or in connection with, a proceeding, or made in connection with an investigation leading to a criminal proceeding (s 69(3))`
    `no: s 69(2) does not apply, because the statement was not made by, or on information supplied by, someone with personal knowledge of the fact (s 69(2))`
    `s 69 does not reach it: it is not a record kept in the course of, or for the purposes of, a business, containing a statement made or recorded there in the course of or for the business (s 69(1)); another exception may apply`

@export Can I use a business record (for example hospital notes, employer records or an insurer's file note) to prove a fact in my claim?
GIVEN `it is a record kept in the course of or for a business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the document is, or was, part of the records of a person, body or organisation kept in the course of or for a business (a business includes a profession, occupation, trade, a hospital or government activity, and a non-profit body)
      `the statement was made or recorded in it in the course of the business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the statement in the document was made or recorded there in the course of, or for the purposes of, that business
      `it was made by or on information from someone with personal knowledge` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the statement was made by someone who had (or might reasonably be supposed to have had) personal knowledge of the fact, or on information supplied directly or indirectly by such a person
      `it was prepared or obtained for a legal proceeding` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the statement was prepared or obtained for the purpose of, in contemplation of, or in connection with a court proceeding (for example a report written for a claim)
      `it was made in connection with an investigation leading to a criminal proceeding` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the statement was made in connection with an investigation relating or leading to a criminal prosecution (for example a police statement)
GIVETH A `Can I use the business record`
`Can I use the business record question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `it is a record kept in the course of or for a business`) OR (NOT `the statement was made or recorded in it in the course of the business`)
    THEN `s 69 does not reach it: it is not a record kept in the course of, or for the purposes of, a business, containing a statement made or recorded there in the course of or for the business (s 69(1)); another exception may apply`
    ELSE IF `it was prepared or obtained for a legal proceeding` OR `it was made in connection with an investigation leading to a criminal proceeding`
         THEN `no: s 69(2) does not apply, because the statement was prepared or obtained for, or in contemplation of or in connection with, a proceeding, or made in connection with an investigation leading to a criminal proceeding (s 69(3))`
         ELSE IF `it was made by or on information from someone with personal knowledge`
              THEN `yes: the hearsay rule does not apply to the record so far as it contains the statement (s 69(2))`
              ELSE `no: s 69(2) does not apply, because the statement was not made by, or on information supplied by, someone with personal knowledge of the fact (s 69(2))`

§§ `ss 76, 78, 79, 80 -- opinions, my expert's included`

-- s 76(1): "Evidence of an opinion is not admissible to prove the existence of a fact about the existence of which the
-- opinion was expressed." s 78: not if "(a) the opinion is based on what the person saw, heard or otherwise perceived about a
-- matter or event; and (b) evidence of the opinion is necessary to obtain an adequate account or understanding of the
-- person's perception". s 79(1): "If a person has specialised knowledge based on the person's training, study or
-- experience, the opinion rule does not apply to evidence of an opinion of that person that is wholly or substantially based
-- on that knowledge." s 80: an opinion "is not inadmissible only because it is about: (a) a fact in issue or an ultimate
-- issue; or (b) a matter of common knowledge."
DECLARE `Can the opinion be used` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the opinion rule does not apply to an opinion wholly or substantially based on the person's specialised knowledge from training, study or experience (s 79(1)), even on the ultimate issue (s 80)`
    `yes: the opinion rule does not apply to a lay opinion based on what the person perceived, where it is needed for an adequate account of that perception (s 78)`
    `no: the opinion rule applies, so the opinion cannot be used to prove the fact it is about (s 76(1)); neither s 78 nor s 79 applies on these facts`

@export Can my expert's (or a witness's) opinion be used to prove a fact in my claim?
GIVEN `the person has specialised knowledge from training, study or experience` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person has specialised knowledge based on their training, study or experience (for example a doctor, engineer or actuary)
      `the opinion is wholly or substantially based on that knowledge` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the opinion is wholly or substantially based on that specialised knowledge (as the court finds)
      `the opinion is based on what the person perceived` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the opinion is based on what the person saw, heard or otherwise perceived about a matter or event
      `the opinion is needed for an adequate account of what they perceived` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether evidence of the opinion is necessary to obtain an adequate account or understanding of what the person perceived (for example "he seemed drunk", "the car was going fast")
GIVETH A `Can the opinion be used`
`Can the opinion be used question` MEANS
    IF `the person has specialised knowledge from training, study or experience` AND `the opinion is wholly or substantially based on that knowledge`
    THEN `yes: the opinion rule does not apply to an opinion wholly or substantially based on the person's specialised knowledge from training, study or experience (s 79(1)), even on the ultimate issue (s 80)`
    ELSE IF `the opinion is based on what the person perceived` AND `the opinion is needed for an adequate account of what they perceived`
         THEN `yes: the opinion rule does not apply to a lay opinion based on what the person perceived, where it is needed for an adequate account of that perception (s 78)`
         ELSE `no: the opinion rule applies, so the opinion cannot be used to prove the fact it is about (s 76(1)); neither s 78 nor s 79 applies on these facts`

§§ `s 177 -- expert certificates (if I want to put my expert's opinion in by certificate)`

-- s 177(1): an expert certificate "signed by the person that: (a) states the person's name and address; and (b) states that
-- the person has specialised knowledge based on his or her training, study or experience, as specified in the certificate;
-- and (c) sets out an opinion that the person holds and that is expressed to be wholly or substantially based on that
-- knowledge." (2): not unless the party "has served on each other party: (a) a copy of the certificate; and (b) a written
-- notice stating that the party proposes to tender the certificate". (3): "Service must be effected not later than: (a) 21
-- days before the hearing; or (b) if, on application by the party before or after service, the court substitutes a different
-- period--the beginning of that period." (5), (6): a party served "may, by written notice ... require the party to call the
-- person who signed the certificate", and then "The expert certificate is not admissible as evidence". (7): costs against a
-- party who, "without reasonable cause", required the person to be called.
-- "21 days before the hearing" is read as the hearing day less 21 days: served on that day or earlier is in time (fork F3).

-- The last day on which I may serve.
@export What is the last day I can serve my expert certificate and notice on the other parties?
GIVEN `the day the hearing starts` IS A DATE @desc The date of the hearing (ISO 8601, e.g. 2027-03-15)
      `the court has substituted a different period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court, on your application, has substituted a period other than 21 days
      `the substituted period in days` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days before the hearing that the court substituted (ignored if the court has not substituted a period)
GIVETH A DATE
`The last day to serve my expert certificate question` MEANS
    `the day` (0 MINUS `the days before the hearing`) `days on from` `the day the hearing starts`
    WHERE
        `the days before the hearing` MEANS
            IF `the court has substituted a different period` THEN `the substituted period in days` ELSE 21

DECLARE `Can I use my expert certificate` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the certificate may be tendered as evidence of the expert's opinion, without calling the expert (s 177(1))`
    `no: the certificate does not state the expert's name and address and specialised knowledge, or set out an opinion expressed to be wholly or substantially based on that knowledge, signed by the expert (s 177(1))`
    `no: I have not served a copy of the certificate and a written notice that I propose to tender it on each other party (s 177(2))`
    `no: I served it too late; service must be no later than 21 days before the hearing, or the start of the period the court substituted (s 177(3))`
    `no: another party has required me by written notice to call the expert, so the certificate is not admissible and I must call the expert to give the opinion (s 177(5), (6))`

@export Can I put my expert's opinion in evidence by certificate instead of calling the expert?
GIVEN `the certificate has everything s 177(1) requires` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the certificate is signed by the expert, states their name and address, states their specialised knowledge based on training, study or experience, and sets out an opinion expressed to be wholly or substantially based on that knowledge
      `I served a copy and a written notice on each other party` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you served on each other party a copy of the certificate and a written notice that you propose to tender it as evidence of the opinion
      `the day I served them` IS A DATE @desc The date you served the certificate and notice (ISO 8601; ignored if not served)
      `the day the hearing starts` IS A DATE @desc The date of the hearing (ISO 8601)
      `the court has substituted a different period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court, on your application, has substituted a period other than 21 days before the hearing
      `the substituted period in days` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of days before the hearing that the court substituted (ignored if none)
      `another party has required me to call the expert` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another party served on you a written notice requiring you to call the expert to give evidence
GIVETH A `Can I use my expert certificate`
`Can I use my expert certificate question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the certificate has everything s 177(1) requires`)
    THEN `no: the certificate does not state the expert's name and address and specialised knowledge, or set out an opinion expressed to be wholly or substantially based on that knowledge, signed by the expert (s 177(1))`
    ELSE IF (NOT `I served a copy and a written notice on each other party`)
         THEN `no: I have not served a copy of the certificate and a written notice that I propose to tender it on each other party (s 177(2))`
         ELSE IF (NOT (`the day I served them` `falls on or before` (`The last day to serve my expert certificate question` `the day the hearing starts` `the court has substituted a different period` `the substituted period in days`)))
              THEN `no: I served it too late; service must be no later than 21 days before the hearing, or the start of the period the court substituted (s 177(3))`
              ELSE IF `another party has required me to call the expert`
                   THEN `no: another party has required me by written notice to call the expert, so the certificate is not admissible and I must call the expert to give the opinion (s 177(5), (6))`
                   ELSE `yes: the certificate may be tendered as evidence of the expert's opinion, without calling the expert (s 177(1))`

DECLARE `Can I make them call their expert` IS ONE OF
    `yes: by written notice served on them I may require them to call the expert, and the certificate is then not admissible (s 177(5), (6)); the Act sets no time for my notice`
    `yes, but at a cost: I may require them to call the expert (s 177(5), (6)), but because the court finds I had no reasonable cause, it may order costs against me (s 177(7))`
    `no: s 177(5) gives that right only to a party on whom the certificate and the notice were served`

@export The other side served me their expert's certificate. Can I make them call the expert instead?
GIVEN `I was served the certificate and their notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were served with a copy of the other side's expert certificate and their written notice that they propose to tender it
      `the court finds I had no reasonable cause to require it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds that you required the expert to be called without reasonable cause
GIVETH A `Can I make them call their expert`
`Can I make them call their expert question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `I was served the certificate and their notice`)
    THEN `no: s 177(5) gives that right only to a party on whom the certificate and the notice were served`
    ELSE IF `the court finds I had no reasonable cause to require it`
         THEN `yes, but at a cost: I may require them to call the expert (s 177(5), (6)), but because the court finds I had no reasonable cause, it may order costs against me (s 177(7))`
         ELSE `yes: by written notice served on them I may require them to call the expert, and the certificate is then not admissible (s 177(5), (6)); the Act sets no time for my notice`

§§ `ss 91, 92 -- judgments, convictions and grants of probate (if one is relevant to my claim)`

-- s 91(1): "Evidence of the decision, or of a finding of fact, in an Australian or overseas proceeding is not admissible to
-- prove the existence of a fact that was in issue in that proceeding." (2): nor usable for that fact "even if it is relevant
-- for another purpose". s 92(2): "In a civil proceeding, subsection 91(1) does not prevent the admission or use of evidence
-- that a party, or a person through or under whom a party claims, has been convicted of an offence, not being a conviction:
-- (a) in respect of which a review or appeal (however described) has been instituted but not finally determined; or (b) that
-- has been quashed or set aside; or (c) in respect of which a pardon has been given." (3): "The hearsay rule and the opinion
-- rule do not apply to evidence of a kind referred to in this section." s 168(3): after notice of conviction evidence, the
-- other party may request (s 166(g): to call a witness from the criminal trial) within 21 days.
DECLARE `Can I use the conviction or finding` IS ONE OF
    `yes: in a civil claim, s 91(1) does not stop me using the conviction of a party, or of a person through or under whom a party claims (s 92(2)), and the hearsay and opinion rules do not apply to it (s 92(3)); once I give notice, the other side has 21 days to make requests about it (s 168(3))`
    `no: the conviction is under review or appeal not yet finally determined, so s 92(2) does not apply and s 91(1) excludes it`
    `no: the conviction has been quashed or set aside, so s 92(2) does not apply and s 91(1) excludes it`
    `no: a pardon has been given for the conviction, so s 92(2) does not apply and s 91(1) excludes it`
    `no: the person convicted is not a party, or a person through or under whom a party claims, so s 92(2) does not apply and s 91(1) excludes it to prove the facts in issue in the criminal case`
    `no: evidence of a decision or finding of fact in another proceeding is not admissible to prove a fact in issue there (s 91(1)), and s 92(2) covers only convictions`

@export Can I use a conviction (for example the other driver's conviction for the crash) or another court's finding to prove my claim?
GIVEN `it is a conviction for an offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether what you want to use is a conviction for an offence (FALSE if it is another decision or finding of fact in another proceeding, such as a civil judgment or tribunal finding)
      `the person convicted is a party, or someone through whom a party claims` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person convicted is a party to your claim (for example the defendant), or a person through or under whom a party claims (for example a deceased whose estate is sued)
      `a review or appeal is pending` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a review or appeal against the conviction has been started but not finally decided
      `the conviction has been quashed or set aside` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conviction has been quashed or set aside
      `a pardon has been given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a pardon has been given for the conviction
GIVETH A `Can I use the conviction or finding`
`Can I use the conviction or finding question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `it is a conviction for an offence`)
    THEN `no: evidence of a decision or finding of fact in another proceeding is not admissible to prove a fact in issue there (s 91(1)), and s 92(2) covers only convictions`
    ELSE IF (NOT `the person convicted is a party, or someone through whom a party claims`)
         THEN `no: the person convicted is not a party, or a person through or under whom a party claims, so s 92(2) does not apply and s 91(1) excludes it to prove the facts in issue in the criminal case`
         ELSE IF `a review or appeal is pending`
              THEN `no: the conviction is under review or appeal not yet finally determined, so s 92(2) does not apply and s 91(1) excludes it`
              ELSE IF `the conviction has been quashed or set aside`
                   THEN `no: the conviction has been quashed or set aside, so s 92(2) does not apply and s 91(1) excludes it`
                   ELSE IF `a pardon has been given`
                        THEN `no: a pardon has been given for the conviction, so s 92(2) does not apply and s 91(1) excludes it`
                        ELSE `yes: in a civil claim, s 91(1) does not stop me using the conviction of a party, or of a person through or under whom a party claims (s 92(2)), and the hearsay and opinion rules do not apply to it (s 92(3)); once I give notice, the other side has 21 days to make requests about it (s 168(3))`

-- s 92(1): "Subsection 91(1) does not prevent the admission or use of evidence of the grant of probate, letters of
-- administration or a similar order of a court to prove: (a) the death, or date of death, of a person; or (b) the due
-- execution of a testamentary document."
DECLARE `Can I use the grant of probate` IS ONE OF
    `yes: a grant of probate, letters of administration or a similar order may be used to prove the person's death or date of death, or the due execution of the will (s 92(1)), and the hearsay and opinion rules do not apply to it (s 92(3))`
    `no: s 92(1) lets a grant prove only death, date of death or due execution of the will; for any other fact in issue in the probate proceeding, s 91(1) excludes it`

@export In a claim about a deceased estate, can I use the grant of probate or letters of administration as proof?
GIVEN `I use it to prove the death or date of death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you use the grant to prove that the person died, or the date of death
      `I use it to prove the will was duly executed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you use the grant to prove that the will (or other testamentary document) was duly executed
GIVETH A `Can I use the grant of probate`
`Can I use the grant of probate question` MEANS
    IF `I use it to prove the death or date of death` OR `I use it to prove the will was duly executed`
    THEN `yes: a grant of probate, letters of administration or a similar order may be used to prove the person's death or date of death, or the due execution of the will (s 92(1)), and the hearsay and opinion rules do not apply to it (s 92(3))`
    ELSE `no: s 92(1) lets a grant prove only death, date of death or due execution of the will; for any other fact in issue in the probate proceeding, s 91(1) excludes it`

§§ `ss 94, 97, 98, 100 -- tendency and coincidence evidence in my claim`

-- s 94(1): Part 3.6 "does not apply to evidence that relates only to the credibility of a witness"; (3): nor to evidence of
-- "the character, reputation or conduct of a person; or ... a tendency" if that "is a fact in issue". s 97(1): tendency
-- evidence "is not admissible to prove that a person has or had a tendency ... unless: (a) the party seeking to adduce the
-- evidence gave reasonable notice in writing to each other party ...; and (b) the court thinks that the evidence will ...
-- have significant probative value." (2): (a) does not apply if adduced under a s 100 direction, or "to explain or
-- contradict tendency evidence adduced by another party". s 98: the same for coincidence evidence. s 100(4): "In a civil
-- proceeding, the party's application may be made without notice of it having been given to one or more of the other
-- parties." s 101 (the further test for the prosecution) is for criminal proceedings only; in a civil claim s 135 remains.
DECLARE `Kind of evidence of other conduct` IS ONE OF
    `tendency evidence: to show a person tends to act in a particular way or have a particular state of mind (s 97)`
    `coincidence evidence: to show from similar events that it is improbable they happened by coincidence (s 98)`

DECLARE `Can I use the tendency or coincidence evidence` IS ONE OF
    `the tendency and coincidence rules do not apply: the evidence goes only to a witness's credibility, or the character, conduct or tendency is itself a fact in issue (s 94(1), (3))`
    `yes: I gave notice (or need not), and the court thinks it will have significant probative value (s 97(1) or s 98(1)); the court may still refuse it under s 135`
    `no: I have not given reasonable written notice to each other party, the court has not directed otherwise (s 100), and I am not using it to explain or contradict the other side's evidence of that kind (s 97(1)(a), (2) or s 98(1)(a), (2)); I may apply for a direction, in a civil claim without notice to the other parties (s 100(4))`
    `no: the court does not think it will have significant probative value (s 97(1)(b) or s 98(1)(b))`

@export Can I use evidence of what the other side did on other occasions (tendency or coincidence evidence) in my civil claim?
GIVEN `the kind of evidence` IS A `Kind of evidence of other conduct` @desc Whether it is tendency evidence (to show the person tends to act a certain way) or coincidence evidence (to show from similar events that a coincidence is improbable)
      `it goes only to a witness's credibility` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the evidence relates only to whether a witness should be believed
      `the conduct or tendency is itself a fact in issue` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person's character, reputation, conduct or tendency is itself one of the facts in issue in your claim
      `I gave each other party reasonable written notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you gave each other party reasonable written notice of your intention to use the evidence
      `the court has directed under s 100` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court, on your application, has directed that the rule does not apply despite your not giving notice
      `I use it to explain or contradict the other side's evidence of that kind` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you use it to explain or contradict tendency or coincidence evidence the other side has adduced
      `the court thinks it will have significant probative value` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court thinks the evidence will, by itself or with your other evidence, have significant probative value
GIVETH A `Can I use the tendency or coincidence evidence`
`Can I use the tendency or coincidence evidence question` MEANS
    IF `it goes only to a witness's credibility` OR `the conduct or tendency is itself a fact in issue`
    THEN `the tendency and coincidence rules do not apply: the evidence goes only to a witness's credibility, or the character, conduct or tendency is itself a fact in issue (s 94(1), (3))`
    ELSE IF (NOT (`I gave each other party reasonable written notice` OR `the court has directed under s 100` OR `I use it to explain or contradict the other side's evidence of that kind`))
         THEN `no: I have not given reasonable written notice to each other party, the court has not directed otherwise (s 100), and I am not using it to explain or contradict the other side's evidence of that kind (s 97(1)(a), (2) or s 98(1)(a), (2)); I may apply for a direction, in a civil claim without notice to the other parties (s 100(4))`
         ELSE IF `the court thinks it will have significant probative value`
              THEN `yes: I gave notice (or need not), and the court thinks it will have significant probative value (s 97(1) or s 98(1)); the court may still refuse it under s 135`
              ELSE `no: the court does not think it will have significant probative value (s 97(1)(b) or s 98(1)(b))`

§§ `ss 118-126 -- my lawyer's advice and documents prepared for my claim (if the other side wants them in evidence)`

-- s 118: evidence is not to be adduced "if, on objection by a client, the court finds that adducing the evidence would
-- result in disclosure of" a confidential communication or document "for the dominant purpose of the lawyer ... providing
-- legal advice to the client". s 119: the same for a confidential communication between the client (or the client's lawyer)
-- and another person, or a confidential document, "for the dominant purpose of the client being provided with professional
-- legal services relating to an Australian or overseas proceeding ..., or an anticipated or pending" one. s 120(1): for "a
-- party who is not represented in the proceeding by a lawyer", a confidential communication or document "for the dominant
-- purpose of preparing for or conducting the proceeding". s 122(1): not if "given with the consent of the client or party";
-- (2), (3): not if the client or party "knowingly and voluntarily disclosed the substance of the evidence to another person"
-- or it was disclosed "with the express or implied consent of the client or party"; (5): not merely because disclosed "in
-- the course of making a confidential communication or preparing a confidential document", "as a result of duress or
-- deception" or "under compulsion of law". Sections 121, 123-126 (deceased client's intentions, enforcing an order, a
-- communication affecting a right, a defendant's use, joint clients, fraud or misconduct, related documents) are asked as
-- one fact.
DECLARE `Dominant purpose of the communication or document` IS ONE OF
    `my lawyer giving me legal advice (s 118)`
    `my being provided with legal services for this or an anticipated proceeding (s 119)`
    `my preparing for or conducting this proceeding myself, without a lawyer (s 120)`
    `some other purpose`

DECLARE `Is it protected` IS ONE OF
    `protected: on my objection, evidence of it is not to be adduced (ss 118, 119 or 120)`
    `protected only if I object: the court decides on my objection; if I do not object, ss 118-120 do not stop it`
    `not protected: it was not confidential, or not made or prepared for a protected dominant purpose (ss 118, 119, 120)`
    `not protected: s 120 protects only a party who is not represented by a lawyer in the proceeding`
    `not protected: I consented to it being adduced (s 122(1))`
    `not protected: I knowingly and voluntarily disclosed its substance, or it was disclosed with my consent, which is inconsistent with objecting (s 122(2), (3))`
    `not protected: another loss of privilege applies (ss 121, 123-126), as the court finds`

@export Can the other side put in evidence what I told my lawyer, or documents prepared for my claim?
GIVEN `it was confidential` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the communication or document was made or prepared in confidence
      `its dominant purpose` IS A `Dominant purpose of the communication or document` @desc The main purpose for which the communication was made or the document prepared: legal advice from your lawyer, legal services for this or an expected court case, your own preparation for the case if you have no lawyer, or some other purpose
      `I am represented by a lawyer in the proceeding` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a lawyer represents you in the claim
      `I object` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you object to the evidence being adduced
      `I consent to it being adduced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you consent to the evidence being adduced
      `I disclosed its substance, or it was disclosed with my consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you knowingly and voluntarily disclosed the substance of the communication or document to another person, or it was disclosed with your express or implied consent
      `that disclosure was only in confidence, under duress or deception, or under compulsion of law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that disclosure was made only in the course of another confidential communication or document, as a result of duress or deception, or because the law compelled it
      `the court finds another loss of privilege applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds that another rule removes the protection: for example fraud or misconduct (s 125), a communication that affects a right (s 121(3)), or related documents (s 126)
GIVETH A `Is it protected`
`Is it protected question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `it was confidential`) OR `its dominant purpose` EQUALS `some other purpose`
    THEN `not protected: it was not confidential, or not made or prepared for a protected dominant purpose (ss 118, 119, 120)`
    ELSE IF `its dominant purpose` EQUALS `my preparing for or conducting this proceeding myself, without a lawyer (s 120)` AND `I am represented by a lawyer in the proceeding`
         THEN `not protected: s 120 protects only a party who is not represented by a lawyer in the proceeding`
         ELSE IF `I consent to it being adduced`
              THEN `not protected: I consented to it being adduced (s 122(1))`
              ELSE IF `I disclosed its substance, or it was disclosed with my consent` AND (NOT `that disclosure was only in confidence, under duress or deception, or under compulsion of law`)
                   THEN `not protected: I knowingly and voluntarily disclosed its substance, or it was disclosed with my consent, which is inconsistent with objecting (s 122(2), (3))`
                   ELSE IF `the court finds another loss of privilege applies`
                        THEN `not protected: another loss of privilege applies (ss 121, 123-126), as the court finds`
                        ELSE IF `I object`
                             THEN `protected: on my objection, evidence of it is not to be adduced (ss 118, 119 or 120)`
                             ELSE `protected only if I object: the court decides on my objection; if I do not object, ss 118-120 do not stop it`

§§ `s 131 -- what was said in settlement negotiations`

-- s 131(1): "Evidence is not to be adduced of: (a) a communication that is made between persons in dispute, or between one or
-- more persons in dispute and a third party, in connection with an attempt to negotiate a settlement of the dispute; or (b) a
-- document (whether delivered or not) that has been prepared in connection with an attempt to negotiate a settlement of a
-- dispute." (2): "Subsection (1) does not apply if: (a) the persons in dispute consent ...; or (b) the substance of the
-- evidence has been disclosed with the express or implied consent of all the persons in dispute; ... (d) the communication or
-- document included a statement to the effect that it was not to be treated as confidential; ... (f) the proceeding ... is a
-- proceeding to enforce an agreement between the persons in dispute to settle the dispute, or a proceeding in which the
-- making of such an agreement is in issue; ... (h) the communication or document is relevant to determining liability for
-- costs". Paragraphs (c), (e), (g), (i), (j), (k) are asked as one fact. (5)(b): a settlement "does not include ... the
-- settlement of a criminal proceeding or an anticipated criminal proceeding".
DECLARE `Can the settlement communication be used` IS ONE OF
    `s 131 does not protect it: it was not made or prepared in connection with an attempt to settle a civil dispute (s 131(1), (5)(b))`
    `protected: evidence of it is not to be adduced (s 131(1))`
    `may be adduced: the persons in dispute consent, or its substance was disclosed with the consent of all of them (s 131(2)(a), (b))`
    `may be adduced: it said it was not to be treated as confidential (s 131(2)(d))`
    `may be adduced: the proceeding is to enforce the settlement agreement, or its making is in issue (s 131(2)(f))`
    `may be adduced on costs: it is relevant to determining liability for costs (s 131(2)(h))`
    `may be adduced: another exception in s 131(2) applies, as the court finds`

@export Can what was said or written in settlement negotiations (for example an offer to settle) be used in evidence in my claim?
GIVEN `it was made or prepared in an attempt to settle the dispute` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the communication or document was made or prepared in connection with an attempt to negotiate a settlement of the dispute (including through a mediator or other third party)
      `the dispute being settled was a criminal proceeding` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the negotiation was about settling a criminal prosecution, actual or anticipated
      `the persons in dispute consent, or its substance was disclosed with all their consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether all the persons in dispute consent to the evidence being used, or its substance has already been disclosed with the express or implied consent of all of them
      `it said it was not to be treated as confidential` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the communication or document included a statement that it was not to be treated as confidential
      `the proceeding is to enforce the settlement or its making is in issue` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceeding is one to enforce a settlement agreement between the persons in dispute, or one in which whether such an agreement was made is in issue
      `it is relevant to who pays costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the communication or document is relevant to deciding liability for costs (for example an offer of compromise that was rejected)
      `the court finds another s 131(2) exception applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds another exception applies: partial disclosure needing full disclosure, contradicting evidence about the negotiations, evidence that would otherwise mislead the court, a communication that affects a right, or fraud, an offence, a civil-penalty act or an abuse of power
GIVETH A `Can the settlement communication be used`
`Can the settlement communication be used question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `it was made or prepared in an attempt to settle the dispute`) OR `the dispute being settled was a criminal proceeding`
    THEN `s 131 does not protect it: it was not made or prepared in connection with an attempt to settle a civil dispute (s 131(1), (5)(b))`
    ELSE IF `the persons in dispute consent, or its substance was disclosed with all their consent`
         THEN `may be adduced: the persons in dispute consent, or its substance was disclosed with the consent of all of them (s 131(2)(a), (b))`
         ELSE IF `it said it was not to be treated as confidential`
              THEN `may be adduced: it said it was not to be treated as confidential (s 131(2)(d))`
              ELSE IF `the proceeding is to enforce the settlement or its making is in issue`
                   THEN `may be adduced: the proceeding is to enforce the settlement agreement, or its making is in issue (s 131(2)(f))`
                   ELSE IF `it is relevant to who pays costs`
                        THEN `may be adduced on costs: it is relevant to determining liability for costs (s 131(2)(h))`
                        ELSE IF `the court finds another s 131(2) exception applies`
                             THEN `may be adduced: another exception in s 131(2) applies, as the court finds`
                             ELSE `protected: evidence of it is not to be adduced (s 131(1))`

§§ `ss 135, 136 -- the court's general discretions over my evidence`

-- s 135: "The court may refuse to admit evidence if its probative value is substantially outweighed by the danger that the
-- evidence might: (a) be unfairly prejudicial to a party; or (b) be misleading or confusing; or (c) cause or result in undue
-- waste of time." s 136: "The court may limit the use to be made of evidence if there is a danger that a particular use of
-- the evidence might: (a) be unfairly prejudicial to a party; or (b) be misleading or confusing." s 137 (a mandatory
-- exclusion) is for prosecution evidence in criminal proceedings only.
DECLARE `Can the court refuse or limit my evidence` IS ONE OF
    `the court may refuse to admit it: its probative value is substantially outweighed by the danger of unfair prejudice, misleading or confusing, or undue waste of time (s 135); that is a discretion, not a duty`
    `the court may not refuse it under s 135, but may limit the use made of it, because a particular use might be unfairly prejudicial or misleading or confusing (s 136)`
    `neither s 135 nor s 136 lets the court refuse or limit it on these findings; in a civil claim there is no mandatory exclusion like s 137`

@export Even if my evidence is admissible, can the court refuse it or limit how it is used?
GIVEN `the court finds its probative value is substantially outweighed by the dangers in s 135` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds the evidence's value in proving a fact is substantially outweighed by the danger that it might be unfairly prejudicial to a party, misleading or confusing, or cause undue waste of time
      `the court finds a particular use might be unfairly prejudicial or misleading` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds there is a danger that a particular use of the evidence might be unfairly prejudicial to a party, or misleading or confusing
GIVETH A `Can the court refuse or limit my evidence`
`Can the court refuse or limit my evidence question` MEANS
    IF `the court finds its probative value is substantially outweighed by the dangers in s 135`
    THEN `the court may refuse to admit it: its probative value is substantially outweighed by the danger of unfair prejudice, misleading or confusing, or undue waste of time (s 135); that is a discretion, not a duty`
    ELSE IF `the court finds a particular use might be unfairly prejudicial or misleading`
         THEN `the court may not refuse it under s 135, but may limit the use made of it, because a particular use might be unfairly prejudicial or misleading or confusing (s 136)`
         ELSE `neither s 135 nor s 136 lets the court refuse or limit it on these findings; in a civil claim there is no mandatory exclusion like s 137`

§§ `ss 160, 161 -- when a letter I posted or an email I sent is presumed received`

-- s 160(1): "It is presumed (unless evidence sufficient to raise doubt about the presumption is adduced) that a postal
-- article sent by prepaid post addressed to a person at a specified address in Australia or in an external Territory was
-- received at that address on the seventh working day after having been posted." (2): not if "(a) the proceeding relates to a
-- contract; and (b) all the parties to the proceeding are parties to the contract; and (c) subsection (1) is inconsistent with
-- a term of the contract." (3): working day excludes Saturday, Sunday and "a public holiday or a bank holiday in the place to
-- which the postal article was addressed".
DECLARE `When is my letter presumed received` IS ONE OF
    `presumed received` HAS `the day presumed received` IS A DATE
    `not presumed: evidence sufficient to raise doubt about the presumption has been adduced, so I must prove receipt otherwise (s 160(1))`
    `not presumed: s 160 covers only a postal article sent by prepaid post to a specified address in Australia or an external Territory`
    `not presumed: the proceeding relates to a contract to which all the parties are parties, and s 160(1) is inconsistent with a term of it (s 160(2))`

@export I posted a letter (for example a notice of my claim). On what day is it presumed to have been received?
GIVEN `it was sent by prepaid post to a specified address in Australia` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you sent it by prepaid post, addressed to the person at a specified address in Australia or an external Territory
      `the day I posted it` IS A DATE @desc The date you posted it (ISO 8601, e.g. 2027-03-15)
      `the public and bank holidays at the address` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc The dates of public holidays and bank holidays in the place the letter was addressed to, in the weeks after posting (an empty list if none)
      `a contract term is inconsistent with the presumption` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim relates to a contract to which all the parties to the claim are parties, and a term of that contract is inconsistent with the seven-working-day presumption
      `evidence raising doubt has been adduced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether evidence sufficient to raise doubt about the letter's receipt on that day has been put before the court
GIVETH A `When is my letter presumed received`
`When is my letter presumed received question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `it was sent by prepaid post to a specified address in Australia`)
    THEN `not presumed: s 160 covers only a postal article sent by prepaid post to a specified address in Australia or an external Territory`
    ELSE IF `a contract term is inconsistent with the presumption`
         THEN `not presumed: the proceeding relates to a contract to which all the parties are parties, and s 160(1) is inconsistent with a term of it (s 160(2))`
         ELSE IF `evidence raising doubt has been adduced`
              THEN `not presumed: evidence sufficient to raise doubt about the presumption has been adduced, so I must prove receipt otherwise (s 160(1))`
              ELSE `presumed received` (`the working day` 7 `after` `the day I posted it` `given the holidays` `the public and bank holidays at the address`)

-- s 161(1): if a document "purports to contain a record of an electronic communication" (other than a lettergram or
-- telegram, s 162), it is presumed (unless evidence sufficient to raise doubt is adduced) that the communication was sent in
-- the form, by or on behalf of the person, on the day, at the time and from the place it appears to have been, "(d) was
-- received at the destination to which it appears from the document to have been sent; and (e) if it appears from the
-- document that the sending of the communication concluded at a particular time--was received at that destination at that
-- time." (2): the same contract exception as s 160(2).
DECLARE `Is my email presumed sent and received` IS ONE OF
    `presumed: it was sent by the person, in the form, on the day, at the time and from the place the record shows, and received at the destination it shows, at the time sending concluded if the record shows it (s 161(1))`
    `not presumed: evidence sufficient to raise doubt about the presumption has been adduced (s 161(1))`
    `not presumed: the proceeding relates to a contract to which all the parties are parties, and the presumption is inconsistent with a term of it (s 161(2))`
    `s 161 does not apply: the document does not purport to contain a record of an electronic communication`

@export Is my email (or other electronic message) presumed to have been sent and received as its record shows?
GIVEN `the document purports to record an electronic communication` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the document you rely on purports to contain a record of an email, text message or other electronic communication
      `a contract term is inconsistent with the presumption` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim relates to a contract to which all the parties to the claim are parties, and a term of that contract is inconsistent with the presumption
      `evidence raising doubt has been adduced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether evidence sufficient to raise doubt about the sending or receipt shown in the record has been put before the court
GIVETH A `Is my email presumed sent and received`
`Is my email presumed sent and received question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the document purports to record an electronic communication`)
    THEN `s 161 does not apply: the document does not purport to contain a record of an electronic communication`
    ELSE IF `a contract term is inconsistent with the presumption`
         THEN `not presumed: the proceeding relates to a contract to which all the parties are parties, and the presumption is inconsistent with a term of it (s 161(2))`
         ELSE IF `evidence raising doubt has been adduced`
              THEN `not presumed: evidence sufficient to raise doubt about the presumption has been adduced (s 161(1))`
              ELSE `presumed: it was sent by the person, in the form, on the day, at the time and from the place the record shows, and received at the destination it shows, at the time sending concluded if the record shows it (s 161(1))`

§§ `ss 68, 168 -- my 21 days to object to, or make requests about, the other side's evidence (if they have given me notice)`

-- The regulative rule. s 68(1): "a party may, not later than 21 days after notice has been given, object to the tender of
-- the evidence". s 168(1): "the other party may only make a request to the party relating to the representation if the
-- request is made within 21 days after the notice was given"; (3): the same after notice of "a person's conviction of an
-- offence in order to prove a fact in issue"; (5), (6): "within 21 days after service of the copy" of a document to be
-- tendered (or of the other document it is to prove). (2), (4), (7): the court may give leave after the 21 days "if it is
-- satisfied that there is good reason to do so". s 68 has no such leave.
-- Both are permissions with a time limit; to keep the step at all I must take it within 21 days, so the rule is written as
-- MUST ... WITHIN 21 with the lapse as the breach. Days are counted from the day of the notice, that day excluded (fork F1),
-- so the 21st day after is the last day.
DECLARE `Claimant` IS ONE OF `the claimant`

DECLARE `Step against the other side's notice` IS ONE OF
    `object to the tender of their hearsay evidence (s 68)`
    `request about a previous representation they gave notice of (s 168(1))`
    `request about a conviction they gave notice of (s 168(3))`
    `request about a document they served a copy of (s 168(5), (6))`

GIVEN `the step` IS A `Step against the other side's notice`
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claimant` `Step against the other side's notice`
`I must take the step within 21 days` `the step` MEANS
    PARTY `the claimant`
    MUST EXACTLY `the step`
    WITHIN 21
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the claimant` BECAUSE "the 21 days after the other side's notice or service passed without the objection or request (Evidence Act 1995 ss 68(1), 168)"

DECLARE `Is my step in time` IS ONE OF
    `in time: I took the step within 21 days after the notice or service`
    `still open` HAS `the last day for the step` IS A DATE
    `too late: the 21 days have passed`
    `too late, but the court has given me leave for good reason (s 168(2), (4), (7))`
    `s 68 gives no objection: the other side's notice does not say they will not call the person because of undue expense or delay or impracticability`

-- Runs the rule over what happened. On the stable l4 a CONSIDER on an EVALTRACE can match FULFILLED but not BREACH: where
-- the step was taken, FULFILLED means in time; where it was not, the helper asks whether taking it today would be in time.
GIVEN `the step`               IS A `Step against the other side's notice`
      `the day of the notice`  IS A DATE
      `the step was taken`     IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day it was taken`   IS A DATE
      `today`                  IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Is my step in time`
`what became of my step` `the step` `the day of the notice` `the step was taken` `the day it was taken` `today` MEANS
    IF `the step was taken`
    THEN CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`I must take the step within 21 days` `the step`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `days to the step`), (EVENT `the claimant` `the step` `days to the step`))
         WHEN FULFILLED THEN `in time: I took the step within 21 days after the notice or service`
         OTHERWISE `too late: the 21 days have passed`
    ELSE CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`I must take the step within 21 days` `the step`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `days to today`), (EVENT `the claimant` `the step` `days to today`))
         WHEN FULFILLED THEN `still open` (`the day` 21 `days on from` `the day of the notice`)
         OTHERWISE `too late: the 21 days have passed`
    WHERE
        `days to the step` MEANS `the days from` `the day of the notice` `to` `the day it was taken`
        `days to today` MEANS `the days from` `the day of the notice` `to` `today`

@export The other side gave me notice that they will use hearsay without calling the person who made the statement. By when can I object?
GIVEN `their notice says calling the person would cause undue expense or delay or be impracticable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other side's notice says they will not call the person who made the statement because it would cause undue expense or undue delay, or would not be reasonably practicable
      `the day their notice was given` IS A DATE @desc The date their notice was given to you (ISO 8601)
      `I have objected` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have given each other party a written notice of objection setting out your grounds
      `the day I objected` IS A DATE @desc The date you gave your written objection (ISO 8601; ignored if you have not objected)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A `Is my step in time`
`By when can I object to their hearsay notice question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `their notice says calling the person would cause undue expense or delay or be impracticable`)
    THEN `s 68 gives no objection: the other side's notice does not say they will not call the person because of undue expense or delay or impracticability`
    ELSE `what became of my step` `object to the tender of their hearsay evidence (s 68)` `the day their notice was given` `I have objected` `the day I objected` `today`

DECLARE `What the other side gave me` IS ONE OF
    `notice that they will use a previous representation (hearsay)`
    `notice that they will use a person's conviction to prove a fact in issue`
    `a served copy of a document they will tender`

@export The other side gave me notice of hearsay or a conviction, or served a copy of a document. By when can I ask them to produce the original, let me examine it, or call the person?
GIVEN `what they gave me` IS A `What the other side gave me` @desc What you received: notice that they will use a previous representation (hearsay), notice that they will use a person's conviction to prove a fact in issue, or a served copy of a document they will tender
      `the day it was given or served` IS A DATE @desc The date the notice was given or the copy served (ISO 8601)
      `I have made my request` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have made your request (to produce or let you examine a document, or to call a witness)
      `the day I made my request` IS A DATE @desc The date you made the request (ISO 8601; ignored if not made)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (ISO 8601)
      `the court has given me leave for good reason` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court, satisfied there is good reason, has given you leave to make the request after the 21 days
GIVETH A `Is my step in time`
`By when can I make my request question` MEANS
    IF `the answer by the clock` EQUALS `too late: the 21 days have passed` AND `the court has given me leave for good reason`
    THEN `too late, but the court has given me leave for good reason (s 168(2), (4), (7))`
    ELSE `the answer by the clock`
    WHERE
        `the step for it` MEANS
            CONSIDER `what they gave me`
            WHEN `notice that they will use a previous representation (hearsay)` THEN `request about a previous representation they gave notice of (s 168(1))`
            WHEN `notice that they will use a person's conviction to prove a fact in issue` THEN `request about a conviction they gave notice of (s 168(3))`
            WHEN `a served copy of a document they will tender` THEN `request about a document they served a copy of (s 168(5), (6))`
        `the answer by the clock` MEANS `what became of my step` `the step for it` `the day it was given or served` `I have made my request` `the day I made my request` `today`

§§ `s 169 -- if the other side does not comply with my request`

-- s 169(1): "If the party has, without reasonable cause, failed or refused to comply with a request, the court may, on
-- application, make one or more of the following orders: (a) an order directing the party to comply with the request; (b) an
-- order that the party produce a specified document or thing, or call as a witness a specified person ...; (c) an order that
-- the evidence in relation to which the request was made is not to be admitted in evidence; (d) such order with respect to
-- adjournment or costs as is just." (2): if they told me "within a reasonable time after receiving the request" that they
-- refuse, "any application ... must be made within a reasonable time after being so informed". (3): if an order under (a) or
-- (b) is not complied with, the court may direct that the evidence not be admitted. (4): reasonable cause includes that "(a)
-- the document or thing to be produced is not available to the party; or (b) the existence and contents of the document are
-- not in issue ...; or (c) the person to be called as a witness is not available."
DECLARE `What can the court do about my request` IS ONE OF
    `the court may, on my application, order them to comply, to produce the document or thing or call the person, that their evidence not be admitted, or as to adjournment or costs (s 169(1)); if they ignore an order to comply or produce, it may exclude their evidence (s 169(3))`
    `nothing yet: they have not failed or refused to comply with my request`
    `no order under s 169(1): the court finds they had reasonable cause (for example the document is not available, its existence and contents are not in issue, or the person is not available, s 169(4))`
    `no order: they told me within a reasonable time that they refuse, and my application was not made within a reasonable time after that (s 169(2))`

@export The other side has not complied with my request about their evidence. What can the court do?
GIVEN `they failed or refused to comply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other side has failed or refused to comply with your request
      `the court finds they had reasonable cause` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds they had reasonable cause, for example the document or thing is not available to them, its existence and contents are not in issue, or the person to be called is not available
      `they told me within a reasonable time that they refuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, within a reasonable time after receiving your request, they told you they refuse to comply
      `the court finds I applied within a reasonable time after being told` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds your application was made within a reasonable time after they told you they refuse (ignored if they did not tell you)
GIVETH A `What can the court do about my request`
`What can the court do about my request question` MEANS
    IF (NOT `they failed or refused to comply`)
    THEN `nothing yet: they have not failed or refused to comply with my request`
    ELSE IF `the court finds they had reasonable cause`
         THEN `no order under s 169(1): the court finds they had reasonable cause (for example the document is not available, its existence and contents are not in issue, or the person is not available, s 169(4))`
         ELSE IF `they told me within a reasonable time that they refuse` AND (NOT `the court finds I applied within a reasonable time after being told`)
              THEN `no order: they told me within a reasonable time that they refuse, and my application was not made within a reasonable time after that (s 169(2))`
              ELSE `the court may, on my application, order them to comply, to produce the document or thing or call the person, that their evidence not be admitted, or as to adjournment or costs (s 169(1)); if they ignore an order to comply or produce, it may exclude their evidence (s 169(3))`

§§ `ss 190, 191 -- waiving the rules of evidence, and agreed facts`

-- s 190(1): "The court may, if the parties consent, by order dispense with the application of any one or more of the
-- provisions of: (a) Division 3, 4 or 5 of Part 2.1; or (b) Part 2.2 or 2.3; or (c) Parts 3.2 to 3.8; in relation to
-- particular evidence or generally." (3): "In a civil proceeding, the court may order that any one or more of the provisions
-- mentioned in subsection (1) do not apply in relation to evidence if: (a) the matter to which the evidence relates is not
-- genuinely in dispute; or (b) the application of those provisions would cause or involve unnecessary expense or delay."
DECLARE `Can the rules of evidence be waived` IS ONE OF
    `yes, by consent: the court may by order dispense with the rules in Divisions 3-5 of Part 2.1, Parts 2.2 and 2.3 and Parts 3.2 to 3.8 (hearsay, opinion, admissions, judgments, tendency, credibility, character), for particular evidence or generally (s 190(1))`
    `yes, without consent: in a civil claim the court may order that those rules do not apply to the evidence, because the matter is not genuinely in dispute or applying them would cause unnecessary expense or delay (s 190(3))`
    `no: the parties do not all consent, and the court does not find the matter is not genuinely in dispute or that the rules would cause unnecessary expense or delay (s 190(1), (3))`

@export Can the court let my evidence in without the usual rules of evidence (for example hearsay or opinion rules)?
GIVEN `all the parties consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether all the parties consent to the court dispensing with the rules for this evidence
      `the court finds the matter is not genuinely in dispute` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds that the matter the evidence relates to is not genuinely in dispute
      `the court finds the rules would cause unnecessary expense or delay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds that applying the rules would cause or involve unnecessary expense or delay
GIVETH A `Can the rules of evidence be waived`
`Can the rules of evidence be waived question` MEANS
    IF `all the parties consent`
    THEN `yes, by consent: the court may by order dispense with the rules in Divisions 3-5 of Part 2.1, Parts 2.2 and 2.3 and Parts 3.2 to 3.8 (hearsay, opinion, admissions, judgments, tendency, credibility, character), for particular evidence or generally (s 190(1))`
    ELSE IF `the court finds the matter is not genuinely in dispute` OR `the court finds the rules would cause unnecessary expense or delay`
         THEN `yes, without consent: in a civil claim the court may order that those rules do not apply to the evidence, because the matter is not genuinely in dispute or applying them would cause unnecessary expense or delay (s 190(3))`
         ELSE `no: the parties do not all consent, and the court does not find the matter is not genuinely in dispute or that the rules would cause unnecessary expense or delay (s 190(1), (3))`

-- s 191(2): "In a proceeding: (a) evidence is not required to prove the existence of an agreed fact; and (b) evidence may not
-- be adduced to contradict or qualify an agreed fact; unless the court gives leave." (3): only if the agreed fact "(a) is
-- stated in an agreement in writing signed by the parties or by Australian legal practitioners ... representing the parties
-- and adduced in evidence in the proceeding; or (b) with the leave of the court, is stated by a party before the court with
-- the agreement of all other parties."
DECLARE `Must I prove the agreed fact` IS ONE OF
    `no: it is an agreed fact, so I need no evidence to prove it, and no party may contradict or qualify it, unless the court gives leave (s 191(2))`
    `yes, with leave: it is an agreed fact, but the court has given leave, so evidence may be required to prove it or adduced to contradict or qualify it (s 191(2))`
    `yes: s 191(2) does not apply, because the agreement is not in a signed writing adduced in evidence, nor stated before the court with all parties' agreement and the court's leave (s 191(3))`

@export The other side has agreed a fact. Do I still have to prove it, and can they go back on it?
GIVEN `it is in a written agreement signed by the parties or their lawyers and put in evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the agreed fact is stated in a written agreement signed by the parties (or their lawyers) and put in evidence in the claim
      `it was stated before the court with all parties' agreement and the court's leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a party stated the fact before the court, with the agreement of all the other parties and the court's leave
      `the court has given leave to prove, contradict or qualify it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has given leave for evidence to prove, contradict or qualify the agreed fact
GIVETH A `Must I prove the agreed fact`
`Must I prove the agreed fact question` MEANS
    IF (NOT (`it is in a written agreement signed by the parties or their lawyers and put in evidence` OR `it was stated before the court with all parties' agreement and the court's leave`))
    THEN `yes: s 191(2) does not apply, because the agreement is not in a signed writing adduced in evidence, nor stated before the court with all parties' agreement and the court's leave (s 191(3))`
    ELSE IF `the court has given leave to prove, contradict or qualify it`
         THEN `yes, with leave: it is an agreed fact, but the court has given leave, so evidence may be required to prove it or adduced to contradict or qualify it (s 191(2))`
         ELSE `no: it is an agreed fact, so I need no evidence to prove it, and no party may contradict or qualify it, unless the court gives leave (s 191(2))`
